-- Passage rg10-context-clues-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('4ba6eec0-ff5c-514b-b9e2-8118638730f9', 'rg10-context-clues-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Why It Mattered$q$, $q$Kwame Mensah had always considered himself a strong writer, so the comment at the top of his history essay stung. "Clear summary," Ms. Okafor had written, "but where is your analysis? You tell me what happened. You never tell me why it mattered."

He reread the essay that evening and had to admit she was right. His paragraphs marched through the building of the old canal in perfect order, but each one stopped at the surface. His treatment of the topic was superficial, touching on every event while examining none of them closely.

The next day he stayed after class. Ms. Okafor pulled a chair beside his desk. "To analyze something," she said, "you break it into parts and ask how each part works. Pick one decision the canal builders made. Then ask what it cost them and what it gave them."

Kwame chose the decision to route the canal around a hill instead of cutting through it. The detour added eleven miles, but it spared the workers a year of digging through solid rock. Once he started asking questions, the paragraph seemed to grow on its own. By the end of the week his draft was twice as long, and for the first time it actually argued something. He planned to revise it twice more before handing in the final copy.

His friend Hana read the new version and was impressed. "You sound so analytical now," she said. "Like a detective sorting through clues instead of a tour guide pointing at buildings."

Kwame laughed, but he kept the comparison. He even wrote it on a note above his desk, a small reminder that listing facts and making sense of them were two different jobs.$q$, 'draft')
